================================================================================
OPEN QUESTIONS
================================================================================

These are recorded rather than resolved. Each one needs a decision from a human
before this attribution can be called complete.

1. Microsoft Writing Style Guide (CC-BY-4.0). The Vale package above is MIT and
   is attributed as such. The style guide it implements is CC-BY-4.0, and
   CC-BY-4.0 requires attribution to the licensor by anyone distributing an
   adaptation. Whether the shipped 86 substitution pairs count as an adaptation
   of the guide, and therefore need a separate attribution to Microsoft, is not
   settled here.

2. ai_tells.json provenance. devtools/build_lint_data.py records no source key
   for this file: the 11 phrases are literals in devtools/builddata/ai_tells.py.
   The attribution above is inferred from two things -- the engine reports
   "tbhb/vale-ai-tells" as the source of every finding from this file
   (src/ste100/checks_lexical.py), and the file's three categories correspond to
   three rules in that package. Whether the list was actually derived from it,
   or written independently and labelled afterwards, cannot be established from
   the repository.

3. MERGED.* lists of unknown provenance. Three generator source keys name lists
   that no longer exist and whose composition is not recorded anywhere in this
   repository:
     - MERGED.wordy_and_complex -- 28 shipped entries in substitutions.json,
       filtered from 876 to those attested by three or more unnamed sources.
     - MERGED.intensifiers_adverbs -- folded into filler.json's
       fillers_and_intensifiers, count not separable.
     - MERGED.weasel_and_hedge -- used only as a build-time cross-check; no
       entries shipped from it.
   Each was a merge of several upstream wordlists whose names were not kept. The
   upstreams may include projects not listed above, and may include projects
   under terms other than MIT or BSD-3-Clause. This cannot be resolved from the
   repository; it needs either the original prose_lint_wordlists.json or a
   decision to rebuild those entries from a known source.

4. filler.json overused_vocabulary. These 40 words are literals in
   devtools/builddata/t6_filler.py with no recorded source. 30 of the 40 also
   appear in vale-ai-tells. The overlap is consistent with independent authoring
   from the same widely-circulated vocabulary, and is not by itself evidence of
   derivation, so no attribution is claimed for this table.

5. Entry counts. The counts above are the counts of shipped entries in this
   repository, taken from src/ste100/data/. They are not upstream list sizes,
   and the generator that produced them cannot be re-run to confirm the mapping
   (see CONTRIBUTING.md).
